.ss 12 0
.SH NAME
cgrun \- a box to start programs from
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cgrun
.RB [ \-\-list ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cgrun
is a small window with a line to type in, a Run button and a Close
button, as the Run box of old had. It is meant to be started by a key
of the window manager or compositor, used once, and gone.
.PP
What is typed is run: the program of that name if there is one, and
otherwise a command line. The programs are those the desktop knows
of, which have a .desktop file that a menu would show, and the name is
the one a menu would show, with capitals or without. A command line is
taken apart into words as a shell takes it, with its quotes and
backslashes, but no shell runs it: there are no variables, patterns or
pipes. A tilde at the start of a word is the home directory. A program
runs in the directory its .desktop file names, and otherwise at home.
.PP
The line finishes itself as it is typed, from the lines that ran
before, the latest first, and failing those from the names of the
programs, the most often started first. What it adds is selected:
typing on replaces it, BackSpace takes it away, and Return runs the
line with it. The lines that ran before can be let down from the
arrow at the end of the line, or gone through with Up and Down.
.PP
The third button, Programs, opens a list beneath: the programs, and
the command lines that ran before. What is typed then narrows the
list instead of finishing itself: every word typed must be in a
program's name, in what kind of thing it says it is, among its
keywords, or be its command. A name that begins with what is typed
comes first, then one with a word that begins with it, then one that
has it anywhere, then the rest; and of two that are met as well, the
one started more often from here comes first. Return starts what is
chosen in the list, and what is typed if nothing in the list is met.
The same button shuts the list again.
.PP
If the program cannot be started, the window stays and says why.
Otherwise it goes. There is one
.B cgrun
on a display: one that is started while another is there takes its
place, so the key always brings it to where the user is.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-list
Start with the list of programs open.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Say how it is used, and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Say which version it is, and exit.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Return
Run the line; with the list open, start what is chosen in it, or the
line if the list is empty.
.TP
.B Control+Return
Run the line as a command line, in a terminal.
.TP
.B Shift+Return
With the list open, run the line as a command line, whatever the list
has.
.TP
.B Up, Down
Go through the lines that ran before; with the list open, choose in
it, as Page Up and Page Down do then. A double click on a row starts
it.
.TP
.B Escape
Go, starting nothing.
.SH TERMINALS
A program whose .desktop file asks for a terminal, and anything
started with the box that says so ticked or with Control+Return, runs
in the terminal that the environment variable
.B TERMINAL
names, which may have arguments of its own; without it, in
.BR cgterm (1)
if that is installed, and else in xterm. The terminal is given
.B \-e
and then the command. A line that ran in a terminal ticks the box when
it comes up again.
.SH WINDOW
The window cannot be resized, which tiling compositors take as a
reason to float it. Its class is Cgrun, which is its application id on
Wayland, for a window rule.
.PP
It is an ordinary window and has the keyboard only as long as the
window manager leaves it there. Where the keyboard follows the
pointer, a rule can keep it; in Hyprland 0.54:
.PP
.nf
  windowrule = match:class ^(Cgrun)$, stay_focused on
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/applications, $XDG_DATA_DIRS/applications
The .desktop files, the user's own before the system's: a file of the
user's with the name of one of the system's stands in its place, and
hides it if it says Hidden. Entries that say NoDisplay, that are for
other desktops than
.BR XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP ,
or whose TryExec is not installed are left out.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/cgrun\-DISPLAY.lock
Locked by the
.B cgrun
there is, for the next one to find it by.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/cgrun/history
How often each program was started from here, and the last fifty
command lines. Without the variable it is under ~/.local/state.
Removing the file forgets it all.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cgterm (1)
